group: C2v
formula: H2O
construction: a central atom and its neighbours
headline: 
source: MO levels: B3LYP, def2-svp · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation in eV · electrons: O 6 (neutral: 2 unpaired e), drawn 6; H2 2 (neutral: 2 unpaired e), drawn 2; model and calculation: 8 drawn + 0 folded (0 levels) = 8 · labels in C<sub>2v</sub>
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (O and H2). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments: above +0.02 bonding, below -0.02 antibonding (*), otherwise non-bonding. A bonding and an antibonding level of the same degeneracy that are the in-phase and out-of-phase combinations of the same fragment orbitals are joined as partners and share a colour; a level without such a partner keeps its own classification.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. Beside each matched level, "overlap" is |<model MO|calculated MO>| (a number from 0 to 1); values below 0.80 are counted in the note under the diagram. Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 8** (O 6, H2 2).\n\n**Construction labels.** sigma, pi and delta are taken relative to each bond from the centre to a ligand atom: sigma = no nodal plane containing the bond axis, pi = one, delta = two (s and a p orbital along the bond have none, a p orbital across it one, a d orbital as many as its m about the bond). For a level made of several ligand orbitals the share on each type is averaged over the bonds, and the label is the dominant one; the hover text gives the split. * marks the out-of-phase combination of the leading fragment orbitals, nb a level with under 5 % on the other fragment. For a diatomic the levels of each sigma/pi, g/u class are named in the textbook order (2s before 2p, bonding before antibonding) whatever their s/p mixing.\n\n**Effect on the bond.** The hover text gives dε/dR, the change of the model level's energy when the bond is stretched, from the model run at the bond length plus and minus 0.02 Å (a central atom: all its bonds together; two fragments: their distance, both rigid). A level that rises on stretching is bonding and strengthens the bond; one that falls weakens it; under 0.5 eV/Å is called little effect.\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in C2v (cross-checked against the code's own C2V labels, which agreed on all 24 orbitals both sides could label, and every computed label subduces to match).  b1 and b2 (b2 and b3 in D2h) depend on the choice of axes; ECCE follows Mulliken's convention, as most textbooks do.
verdict: differs The model explains the calculated occupied orbitals in the right order. The empty levels differ: the calculated LUMO is 4a1, the model's is 2b2*.
